341
00:19:41.640 --> 00:19:42.720
I want to talk a little bit

342
00:19:42.720 --> 00:19:47.000
about one of the pieces of Embabel, which is the planner.

343
00:19:47.040 --> 00:19:48.480
And this is, this is super interesting.

344
00:19:48.480 --> 00:19:51.200
So it's it's a pathfinding algorithm.

345
00:19:51.200 --> 00:19:53.360
The goat, the goat goat.

346
00:19:53.360 --> 00:19:54.960
Goal oriented action planning.

347
00:19:54.960 --> 00:19:57.040
That's it. Goal oriented action planning.

348
00:19:57.040 --> 00:20:02.840
So it's a pathfinding algorithm that was originally built for NPCs in gaming.

349
00:20:02.880 --> 00:20:03.520
Is that right?

350
00:20:03.520 --> 00:20:04.240
It is.

351
00:20:04.240 --> 00:20:07.360
Yes. And the key thing is it's it's deterministic.

352
00:20:07.400 --> 00:20:08.360
Correct.

353
00:20:08.360 --> 00:20:12.560
And I think if you look at other frameworks,

354
00:20:12.680 --> 00:20:16.800
whether it's Lange chain or AI or those types of things, it's the it's

355
00:20:16.800 --> 00:20:22.200
ultimately the LM that effectively helps decide what the the what to do

356
00:20:22.200 --> 00:20:25.320
next, what the planning aspects are and things like that.

357
00:20:25.320 --> 00:20:28.760
I'd love to talk about why you picked

358
00:20:28.800 --> 00:20:32.920
the this algorithm from the NPC space.

359
00:20:32.920 --> 00:20:34.160
What did you see there?

360
00:20:34.160 --> 00:20:37.720
I started off thinking about some of the most obvious ways to do it,

361
00:20:37.720 --> 00:20:40.080
and one of the most obvious ways to do it is the state machine.

362
00:20:40.080 --> 00:20:40.960
So land graph.

363
00:20:40.960 --> 00:20:44.560
In fairness to land graph, land graph is deterministic.

364
00:20:44.560 --> 00:20:47.880
You define a state machine ahead of time.

365
00:20:47.880 --> 00:20:50.560
So I started off by using that approach.

366
00:20:50.560 --> 00:20:51.680
But let's let's step back

367
00:20:51.680 --> 00:20:55.920
a little bit first and talk about the motivation for planning.

368
00:20:55.920 --> 00:21:02.240
So obviously you could just give your model a bunch of tools right.

369
00:21:02.280 --> 00:21:05.200
And let the loop handle things.

370
00:21:05.200 --> 00:21:08.760
And there are cases where that makes sense.

371
00:21:08.800 --> 00:21:12.440
But fundamentally for things like automating a business process

372
00:21:12.440 --> 00:21:16.720
in a consistent, predictable way, that really isn't sufficient

373
00:21:16.720 --> 00:21:21.440
because you don't know what order the LM will invoke, the tools you give it in.

374
00:21:21.480 --> 00:21:26.440
You don't know precisely what arguments it will give it to them.

375
00:21:26.440 --> 00:21:29.440
So the idea is that we

376
00:21:29.440 --> 00:21:33.440
enable users to decompose flows into a number of steps.

377
00:21:33.440 --> 00:21:38.080
Those steps might invoke one or more llms, or they might just be code steps.

378
00:21:38.080 --> 00:21:41.400
So to that extent, these are things that Lang graph does that

379
00:21:41.760 --> 00:21:44.120
AI does that Microsoft semantic kernel does.

380
00:21:44.120 --> 00:21:47.160
I mean, that's a familiar idea and a proven idea

381
00:21:47.160 --> 00:21:49.960
that you break things down into smaller steps.

382
00:21:49.960 --> 00:21:53.320
It also means that if you're using Llms in those steps,

383
00:21:53.320 --> 00:21:56.360
you can use different llms for different requirements.

384
00:21:56.360 --> 00:22:00.840
And that means, for example, you could potentially even use local LLM

385
00:22:00.920 --> 00:22:02.480
behind your own firewall

386
00:22:02.480 --> 00:22:05.520
for certain things that are small enough for local them to do it.

387
00:22:05.520 --> 00:22:06.520
And highly sensitive.

388
00:22:06.520 --> 00:22:08.880
So your customer data never goes outside.

389
00:22:08.880 --> 00:22:10.200
So there's a lot of benefits there.

390
00:22:10.200 --> 00:22:14.280
But it brings us back to how do we how do we plan those steps?

391
00:22:14.280 --> 00:22:17.280
How do we decide what order we're going to do things.

392
00:22:17.440 --> 00:22:20.320
So you can do the state machine idea.

393
00:22:20.320 --> 00:22:22.680
But what I found when I was working on something

394
00:22:22.680 --> 00:22:27.520
pretty similar to what came out with is firstly

395
00:22:27.840 --> 00:22:31.480
changing a state machine, for example, adding more states and transitions.

396
00:22:31.480 --> 00:22:32.600
It's pretty fiddly, right?

397
00:22:32.600 --> 00:22:36.000
You've got to rewire the thing if you're extending it.

398
00:22:36.000 --> 00:22:39.720
And secondly, you have a problem with respect to typing,

399
00:22:39.720 --> 00:22:43.160
because your state transitions are generally orthogonal

400
00:22:43.200 --> 00:22:47.760
to the type requirements of the individual action states.

401
00:22:47.800 --> 00:22:52.560
So our idea with goat planning differs in two ways.

402
00:22:52.600 --> 00:22:55.400
Firstly, it is dynamic.

403
00:22:55.400 --> 00:23:01.280
So the planning occurs at runtime, and secondly it is

404
00:23:02.240 --> 00:23:04.320
totally integrated with the type system.

405
00:23:04.320 --> 00:23:07.320
So we allow people to create custom

406
00:23:08.640 --> 00:23:09.800
custom conditions.

407
00:23:09.800 --> 00:23:15.240
But fundamentally the ordering of actions is typically defined by the

408
00:23:15.600 --> 00:23:19.600
the arguments and return types of Java methods,

409
00:23:19.640 --> 00:23:23.920
which means that you can never have an action that's invoked when it doesn't have

410
00:23:23.960 --> 00:23:28.240
the parameters that are needed for it at the time.

411
00:23:28.240 --> 00:23:31.800
So go is essentially it's an A star algorithm.

412
00:23:31.800 --> 00:23:34.760
So we identify a goal.

413
00:23:34.760 --> 00:23:39.960
We look at the steps that can get from the current world state to the goal.

414
00:23:39.960 --> 00:23:41.880
We do that by chaining actions.

415
00:23:41.880 --> 00:23:44.120
So a goal has preconditions.

416
00:23:44.120 --> 00:23:48.920
So certain, you know conditions in the world state that must be true.

417
00:23:49.200 --> 00:23:53.840
Actions have preconditions and expected post conditions.

418
00:23:53.840 --> 00:23:56.760
So preconditions are absolute.

419
00:23:56.760 --> 00:23:58.200
You cannot declare your goal

420
00:23:58.200 --> 00:24:00.680
to be satisfied unless those conditions have been met.

421
00:24:00.680 --> 00:24:04.160
You cannot invoke any action unless those conditions have been met.

422
00:24:04.160 --> 00:24:09.640
But the post conditions of actions are essentially promises

423
00:24:09.680 --> 00:24:12.680
that I will have achieved this essentially side effect.

424
00:24:12.760 --> 00:24:16.640
So the planner starts off by identifying

425
00:24:16.640 --> 00:24:20.040
a plan from the current world state to the desired goal.

426
00:24:20.040 --> 00:24:22.000
And of course, it may say there is no plan

427
00:24:22.000 --> 00:24:25.760
which is legitimate and good to know the.

428
00:24:25.760 --> 00:24:29.680
So it won't do things that don't make sense,

429
00:24:29.720 --> 00:24:32.600
then it will execute the first action.

430
00:24:32.600 --> 00:24:35.400
It will identify a list of actions that could achieve that goal.

431
00:24:35.400 --> 00:24:37.280
We'll execute the first action.

432
00:24:37.280 --> 00:24:39.640
It will now replan.

433
00:24:39.640 --> 00:24:43.600
So it will look at the world state as it stands after the execution

434
00:24:43.600 --> 00:24:46.600
of each action and say, okay, how do I get to the goal now?

435
00:24:46.800 --> 00:24:49.960
Like most of the time you're the happy path will work.

436
00:24:50.000 --> 00:24:52.760
The action promised that it will have certain,

437
00:24:52.760 --> 00:24:55.800
you know, certain things will be true after it's executed.

438
00:24:55.800 --> 00:24:57.800
The planner checks. Yep, looks like the world states

439
00:24:57.800 --> 00:25:00.920
what I expected and it will go to the next step.

440
00:25:00.920 --> 00:25:02.560
And this is entirely automated.

441
00:25:02.560 --> 00:25:03.960
This is entirely automated.

442
00:25:03.960 --> 00:25:09.200
So typically Java developers don't really need to

443
00:25:09.880 --> 00:25:14.600
know about the guts of the planner, typically, the way in which they provide

444
00:25:14.600 --> 00:25:19.080
inputs to the planner is they define what we call action methods.

445
00:25:19.080 --> 00:25:25.080
So they annotate Java methods with that action and the arguments to those methods.

446
00:25:25.080 --> 00:25:29.240
And the return types basically give the planner information

447
00:25:29.240 --> 00:25:31.000
to perform the chaining.

448
00:25:31.000 --> 00:25:36.120
There are some cases where you also want custom conditions,

449
00:25:36.120 --> 00:25:41.600
so they can define custom conditions to further control the workflow.

450
00:25:41.600 --> 00:25:44.960
But it means, for example, that there may be

451
00:25:44.960 --> 00:25:48.080
more than one route to a goal, and it can choose the cheapest route

452
00:25:48.080 --> 00:25:52.160
because you can assign cost to individual actions.

453
00:25:52.200 --> 00:25:53.800
Cost can even be dynamic.

454
00:25:53.800 --> 00:25:58.560
So if one of your actions involve, say, invoking a system that might be under

455
00:25:58.560 --> 00:26:02.800
heavy load, you could actually reflect that in a dynamic cost value for that.

456
00:26:02.800 --> 00:26:06.560
So it might automatically choose a different route.

457
00:26:07.000 --> 00:26:08.000
That's super interesting.

458
00:26:08.000 --> 00:26:10.040
And that's and that's dynamic at runtime.

459
00:26:10.040 --> 00:26:13.440
Not it can kind of like decide based on the current state of the world.

460
00:26:14.000 --> 00:26:15.720
That's pretty cool.

461
00:26:15.720 --> 00:26:18.680
One of the other things, because it's very deterministic.

462
00:26:18.680 --> 00:26:21.840
If you were to ask

463
00:26:21.880 --> 00:26:24.880
why did why was the decision made?

464
00:26:24.920 --> 00:26:28.920
What can what can you provide as, as, you know, some information

465
00:26:28.920 --> 00:26:33.360
or diagnostic about why something happened that non-deterministic planner

466
00:26:33.360 --> 00:26:34.360
wouldn't be able to?

467
00:26:34.360 --> 00:26:39.240
Yes. We can absolutely show you the plan that was formulated, the

468
00:26:39.280 --> 00:26:45.040
what we saw in the world state that caused us to formulate that plan.

469
00:26:45.080 --> 00:26:50.360
The planner and the whole of In Embabel emits a whole lot of events.

470
00:26:50.360 --> 00:26:52.160
So you can also write listeners

471
00:26:52.160 --> 00:26:56.880
that will persist these or whatever you want to do with your audit logs.

472
00:26:56.880 --> 00:27:01.040
So yes, you can absolutely explain why it did a thing

473
00:27:01.040 --> 00:27:04.800
and ensure that it does the same thing every time.

474
00:27:04.840 --> 00:27:08.920
Of course, once you get inside the action steps, if you're calling an LLM

475
00:27:09.120 --> 00:27:13.400
that may, that's not going to be wholly deterministic.

476
00:27:13.400 --> 00:27:18.440
But on the other hand, this enables you to right size your invocations.

477
00:27:18.440 --> 00:27:22.760
So if you have, as you unfortunately need to do with a bot

478
00:27:23.000 --> 00:27:26.400
like three page prompt and 32,

479
00:27:27.360 --> 00:27:29.680
you're never going to have total predictability.

480
00:27:29.680 --> 00:27:34.640
Whereas if you're doing something with a very small prompt and three tools.

481
00:27:35.360 --> 00:27:37.400
It's going to be highly predictable.

482
00:27:37.400 --> 00:27:38.160
I mean, it's obviously

483
00:27:38.160 --> 00:27:41.960
not going to be 100% predictable, but you will find that the amount of time

484
00:27:41.960 --> 00:27:47.760
you need to expend on prompt engineering is way, way lower.

485
00:27:47.760 --> 00:27:50.920
So yes, overall, the systems tend to become much

486
00:27:50.920 --> 00:27:54.200
more explainable, much more deterministic.

487
00:27:54.800 --> 00:27:55.600
Absolutely.

